After the Civil War, one must note the ruined physical and economical conditions in the South. Infrastructure such as railroads, bridges, and buildings were destroyed or neglected, leading to a significant decline in the Southern economy. The agricultural system, which relied heavily on slave labor, was in disarray, and many plantations were abandoned. 

The Northern army caused significant economic damage to the Southern property, and the dissolution of the Southern plantation economy and Confederate government backing the notes, confederate bonds and currency lost recognition. The Republican governments built roads, bridges, and railroads and established orphanages and institutions for the care of the mentally ill and disabled. They also created the first public school systems.

However, in order to reconstruct public infrastructure, taxes raised significantly in the South, slowing the recovery further. 

